# Chapter 3 — The Screen

So far the console has answered you in words and numbers. Now we turn on the
part that answers in *light*. Motoi has a screen, and the screen is not a
mystery — it is a small, honest grid of dots you can write to one at a time.
Once you know its shape, everything you will ever draw is just a pattern of
those dots.

## The screen is 128 by 128, and it counts from zero

The Motoi screen is **128 pixels wide and 128 pixels tall**. That is the whole
canvas — small on purpose, the way the classic fantasy consoles were, because
a small screen is a screen you can hold in your head. Every pixel has an
address: an `x` that runs left to right and a `y` that runs top to bottom. Both
start at **0**, not 1.

```
        x = 0                     x = 127
   y=0  +---------------------------+
        | (0,0) is the top-left     |
        |                           |
        |          (64,64)          |
        |          the middle       |
        |                           |
 y=127  +---------------------------+
```

The top-left corner is `(0, 0)`. The bottom-right is `(127, 127)`. The middle
is roughly `(64, 64)`. `y` growing **downward** trips people up at first —
remember that the screen is written like a page, top line first.

Before you draw anything, load the screen library:

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
```

`framebuffer` is the console's word for the screen's memory — the grid of
color values that gets painted every frame. From here on, `fb/` verbs write
into it.

## Light one pixel

The smallest thing you can do to a screen is turn on a single dot. `fb/pixel`
takes an `x`, a `y`, and a **color number**, and sets that one pixel:

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(fb/pixel 64 64 8)
(display 'lit)
(newline)
```

```
lit
```

You just set the pixel at the middle of the screen to color `8`. It ran, and
it answered `lit` so you know the line completed. On a real Motoi screen you
would now see one red dot dead center. That is the atom of all screen drawing:
**pick a spot, pick a color, set the dot.** Everything larger is many of these.

## The sixteen colors

Motoi's screen speaks **16 colors**, numbered `0` through `15`. You do not mix
red-green-blue values pixel by pixel — you pick from a fixed, named palette,
exactly like the old consoles. That constraint is a gift: sixteen good colors
that always look right together beat sixteen million that mostly do not.

```
  0 black        4 brown        8 red         12 blue
  1 dark-blue    5 dark-grey    9 orange      13 lavender
  2 dark-purple  6 light-grey  10 yellow      14 petal
  3 dark-green   7 white       11 green       15 peach
```

The screen starts filled with color `0` (black). Every dot you set replaces the
black underneath it. When you want to know what a color number looks like, the
fastest answer is the oldest one in this book: set a pixel to it at the prompt
and look.

## Draw a filled rectangle

You will rarely place pixels one at a time — that is the atom, not the tool.
`fb/rect` fills a whole block in one call: an `x`, a `y`, a width, a height,
and a color.

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(fb/rect 48 48 32 32 12)
(display 'drew-a-square)
(newline)
```

```
drew-a-square
```

That paints a **32 by 32 blue square** whose top-left corner sits at `(48, 48)`
— so it lands centered-ish on the screen. One call, a few hundred pixels set.
This is the shape of screen work: you describe a region and a color, and the
console fills every dot inside it for you.

## Put words on the screen

A screen that can only draw blocks is half a screen. `fb/text` writes
characters into the framebuffer at a pixel position, in a color of your choice:

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(fb/rect 0 0 128 12 1)
(fb/text 4 2 \"MOTOI\" 7)
(display 'titled)
(newline)
```

```
titled
```

You painted a dark-blue bar across the top (`fb/rect ... 1`), then wrote
`MOTOI` in white (`fb/text ... 7`) two pixels down from the corner. Layering
like this — a background block, then text on top — is how every title bar,
score readout, and menu label in every game you build gets made.

## Look at what you drew

The screen lives in memory until something paints it. When you are at the
prompt and want to *see* the framebuffer as it stands, `fb/dump` renders the
whole 128×128 grid as a block of colored cells you can read right there in the
terminal:

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(fb/rect 40 40 48 48 11)
(fb/text 50 60 \"HI\" 0)
(fb/dump)
(display 'shown)
(newline)
```

```
shown
```

`fb/dump` is your screen's mirror: it takes everything you have drawn — the
green square, the black `HI` inside it — and shows it to you as a picture. In a
full Motoi session the screen repaints on its own every frame; `fb/dump` is the
manual version, for when you are sketching at the prompt and want to check your
work without waiting for a frame.

## What you just proved

You learned the screen's true shape — 128×128, counting from zero, `y` growing
downward — met the sixteen-color palette, and drew with all four of the screen
verbs: a pixel, a rectangle, some text, and a full dump to look at the result.
Every one of those programs ran. You now have the entire vocabulary of the flat
screen. What is missing is not more verbs — it is *a thing to draw.* Next
chapter gives the screen its first real inhabitant: a sprite.

> **Tip**
>
> Drawing off the edge of the screen is not an error — pixels outside 0..127
> are simply clipped away. That means you can move a big shape past the edge
> and let the screen trim it, which is exactly how things slide on and off in a
> game. Try `(fb/rect 120 40 40 20 9)` and note the console does not complain.

> **Warning**
>
> The screen is 16 colors, `0` to `15`. Color numbers outside that range wrap
> or clamp rather than doing what you meant — if a shape comes out the wrong
> color, check that your color number is in range before suspecting anything
> deeper. Appendix C lays out the framebuffer and its palette in full.

